keeberry bundles the following third-party flasher as a Tauri `externalBin`
sidecar (see tauri.conf.json `bundle.externalBin`):

  wb32-dfu-updater_cli
  Upstream: https://github.com/WestberryTech/wb32-dfu-updater
  License:  Apache-2.0

  The committed binary `wb32-dfu-updater_cli-<target-triple>` is the Homebrew
  build (`/opt/homebrew/bin/wb32-dfu-updater_cli`). A copy of the Apache-2.0
  license text is required in any redistributed bundle; obtain it from the
  upstream repository.

PACKAGING FOLLOW-UP — libusb (REQUIRED before shipping a distributable .app)

  `otool -L` on the sidecar reports a non-portable dynamic dependency:

    /opt/homebrew/opt/libusb/lib/libusb-1.0.0.dylib   (Homebrew, absolute path)
    /usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib                        (system, fine)

  The libusb path resolves only on a machine that has Homebrew's libusb
  installed, so the sidecar will fail to launch from a bundle on a clean Mac.
  Before distribution, ONE of the following must be done (not yet done here):

    1. Bundle the dylib: copy libusb-1.0.0.dylib into the app bundle (e.g.
       Contents/Frameworks) and rewrite the sidecar's load command with
       `install_name_tool -change <abs path> @executable_path/../Frameworks/...`
       (and re-sign), or
    2. Ship a statically-linked `wb32-dfu-updater_cli` (no libusb dylib dep).

  This is a real macOS packaging step. The sidecar runs as-is on a dev machine
  that has `brew install libusb`; the bundling/static-link work is tracked here
  so it is not mistaken for complete.
